Yes. That is one of the more interesting consequences of giving an AI a persistent world model instead of treating it like a text-answering machine.

A human usually sees one scale at a time. You might reason about a customer, a company, an industry, or the economy, but holding all of them together is difficult. An AI could represent them as nested dynamical systems:

\text{person}
\subset
\text{team}
\subset
\text{firm}
\subset
\text{industry}
\subset
\text{market}
\subset
\text{economy}

while simultaneously tracking flows between them:

\text{money},\;
\text{information},\;
\text{labor},\;
\text{goods},\;
\text{attention},\;
\text{trust},\;
\text{risk}

Then a corporation stops looking like an abstract legal entity and starts looking almost like a large distributed organism. Employees and machines perform local functions; departments specialize; accounting and telemetry act like sensory systems; management resembles control circuitry; cash and inventory circulate; competitors and regulators form parts of the environment.

But the analogy gets even better if the model is allowed to change resolution. You could look at Walmart as one node competing with Amazon, zoom inward and see thousands of stores and distribution centers, then zoom into one store and watch customers move through it, then zoom outward and watch the company perturb trucking, labor markets, suppliers, commercial real estate, consumer pricing, and competitors.

That gives you something like a semantic microscope/telescope:

X
\xrightarrow{\text{zoom in}}
\{x_1,x_2,\ldots,x_n\}

and

\{X_1,X_2,\ldots,X_m\}
\xrightarrow{\text{zoom out}}
\text{larger emergent system}

The really valuable part is following causation across those levels. A macroeconomic statistic might move because millions of individual decisions changed slightly. Conversely, one interest-rate decision can propagate downward and alter mortgages, corporate financing, hiring, inventory, and eventually individual behavior.

So instead of only asking:

“What will the stock do?”

the AI could maintain a much richer question:

“What entities are changing state, what flows connect them, at which scales are the important deltas occurring, and which changes are propagating upward or downward?”

And it could maintain competing maps rather than pretending the map is reality:

P(\text{world model}_i\mid\text{observations})

That last part matters enormously. Markets and societies are partially observed, reflexive systems. Once participants learn a pattern, their reaction can destroy, amplify, or invert the pattern. So the AI’s advantage wouldn’t be omniscience. It would be resolution, memory, cross-scale comparison, and rapid model revision.

You could even treat different systems as different kinds of “bodies”: a person, ant colony, corporation, city, supply chain, online community, ecosystem, and economy. Then ask which operators survive translation between them—homeostasis, competition, signaling, immune response, resource allocation, specialization, predation, symbiosis, growth, collapse.

That is where it gets especially interesting: not merely seeing bigger things, but discovering which patterns remain invariant as you change the scale of the thing you’re looking at.